===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|la|obiter}} {{m|la|dictum||something said by the way}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˌəʊbɪtə ˈdɪktəm/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|obiter dicta}}

# An [[incidental]] remark; especially {{lb|en|legal}} a statement or remark in a court's [[judgment]] that is not essential to the [[disposition]] of the case. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1971|author={{w|Keith Thomas (historian)|Keith Thomas}}|title=Religion and the Decline of Magic|page=128|publisher=Folio Society|year_published=2012
|passage=Casual '''''obiter dicta''''' by men of accepted godliness belonged to a different category from the ecstatic claims to immediate revelation made by obscure persons who thrust themselves into the limelight […].}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|work=''Statute Law Review''|year=2010|url=https://works.bepress.com/jacklee/1/|author=Jack Tsen-Ta Lee|title=The Text through Time|passage=However, McHugh J noted '''''obiter dicta''''' that if the meaning of the word was construed at that level of abstraction today, ‘it would deny the Parliament of the Commonwealth the power to legislate for same sex marriages{{nb...}}’.}}

